Description: CVE-2026-41991 - gzexe: use 'set -C' to avoid TOCTOU when mktemp is missing
 GNU gzip's gzexe utility used a PID-only temp filename and a non-exclusive
 redirect when mktemp was unavailable, allowing a local symlink race
 (CWE-377). Backport the upstream fix to use 'set -C' (noclobber) plus a
 restrictive umask so the redirect fails atomically if the target exists.
 The same hardened fallback is applied to zdiff for consistency.
 .
 Upstream also shipped a later typo-only follow-up (6da5a7b2) for a
 diagnostic introduced after 1.12; that hunk is not applicable to
 Debian bookworm gzip 1.12-1 (message already correct) and is omitted.
Origin: upstream, https://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/gzip.git/commit/?id=4e6f8b24ab823146ab8776f0b7fe486ab34d4269
Bug: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-41991
Author: Paul Eggert <eggert@cs.ucla.edu>
Last-Update: 2026-08-03
---
--- a/gzexe.in
+++ b/gzexe.in
@@ -128,6 +128,7 @@ for i do
     tmp=`mktemp "${dir}gzexeXXXXXXXXX"`
   else
     tmp=${dir}gzexe$$
+    (umask 77; set -C; > "$tmp")
   fi && { cp -p "$file" "$tmp" 2>/dev/null || cp "$file" "$tmp"; } || {
     res=$?
     printf >&2 '%s\n' "$0: cannot copy $file"
--- a/zdiff.in
+++ b/zdiff.in
@@ -157,12 +157,11 @@ case $file2 in
                           *) TMPDIR=/tmp/;;
                         esac
                         if type mktemp >/dev/null 2>&1; then
-                          tmp=`mktemp "${TMPDIR}zdiffXXXXXXXXX"` ||
-                            exit 2
+                          tmp=`mktemp "${TMPDIR}zdiffXXXXXXXXX"`
                         else
-                          set -C
                           tmp=${TMPDIR}zdiff$$
-                        fi
+                          (umask 77; set -C; > "$tmp")
+                        fi &&
                         'gzip' -cdfq -- "$file2" > "$tmp" || exit 2
                         gzip_status=$(
                           exec 4>&1
Description: CVE-2026-41992 - gzip: don't mishandle .lzh after .Z
 GNU gzip has a global buffer over-read in its LZH decompression logic.
 The `left` and `right` Huffman-tree arrays used by unlzh.c are shared
 with the LZ77/LZW decompression routines and are not reinitialized
 between files processed in the same invocation. An attacker can craft
 an LZW (.Z) file followed by an LZH (.lzh) file processed in a single
 `gzip -d` invocation to poison the shared state; the LZH decoder in
 read_c_len then follows stale values left in the shared array, causing
 reads past the end of the allocated global buffer.
 .
 Fixed upstream by clearing left/right in unlzh.c read_c_len when n == 0.
Origin: upstream, https://cgit.git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/gzip.git/commit/?id=63dbf6b3b9e6e781df1a6a64e609b10e23969681
Author: Paul Eggert <eggert@cs.ucla.edu>
Last-Update: 2026-07-01

--- a/unlzh.c
+++ b/unlzh.c
@@ -242,6 +242,12 @@
         c = getbits(CBIT);
         for (i = 0; i < NC; i++) c_len[i] = 0;
         for (i = 0; i < 4096; i++) c_table[i] = c;
+
+        /* Needed in case LEFT and RIGHT are reused from a previous
+           LZW decompression.  It may be overkill to clear all of both
+           arrays, but nobody has had time to analyze this carefully.  */
+        memzero(left, (2 * NC - 1) * sizeof *left);
+        memzero(right, (2 * NC - 1) * sizeof *left);
     } else {
         i = 0;
         while (i < n) {
